@anon.152f57749fcdf0a673d809c4f4545e7f.725 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2342 x i8] }> <{ [2342 x i8] c"A reference to a label or bibliography.\0A\0AProduces a textual reference to a label. For example, a reference to a\0Aheading will yield an appropriate string such as \22Section 1\22 for a reference\0Ato the first heading. The references are also links to the respective\0Aelement. Reference syntax can also be used to [cite] from a bibliography.\0A\0AReferenceable elements include [headings]($heading), [figures]($figure),\0A[equations]($math.equation), and [footnotes]($footnote). To create a custom\0Areferenceable element like a theorem, you can create a figure of a custom\0A[`kind`]($figure.kind) and write a show rule for it. In the future, there\0Amight be a more direct way to define a custom referenceable element.\0A\0AIf you just want to link to a labelled element and not get an automatic\0Atextual reference, consider using the [`link`] function instead.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#set heading(numbering: \221.\22)\0A#set math.equation(numbering: \22(1)\22)\0A\0A= Introduction <intro>\0ARecent developments in\0Atypesetting software have\0Arekindled hope in previously\0Afrustrated researchers. @distress\0AAs shown in @results, we ...\0A\0A= Results <results>\0AWe discuss our approach in\0Acomparison with others.\0A\0A== Performance <perf>\0A@slow demonstrates what slow\0Asoftware looks like.\0A$ O(n) = 2^n $ <slow>\0A\0A#bibliography(\22works.bib\22)\0A```\0A\0A# Syntax\0AThis function also has dedicated syntax: A reference to a label can be\0Acreated by typing an `@` followed by the name of the label (e.g.\0A`[= Introduction <intro>]` can be referenced by typing `[@intro]`).\0A\0ATo customize the supplement, add content in square brackets after the\0Areference: `[@intro[Chapter]]`.\0A\0A# Customization\0AIf you write a show rule for references, you can access the referenced\0Aelement through the `element` field of the reference. The `element` may\0Abe `{none}` even if it exists if Typst hasn't discovered it yet, so you\0Aalways need to handle that case in your code.\0A\0A```example\0A#set heading(numbering: \221.\22)\0A#set math.equation(numbering: \22(1)\22)\0A\0A#show ref: it => {\0A  let eq = math.equation\0A  let el = it.element\0A  if el != none and el.func() == eq {\0A    // Override equation references.\0A    numbering(\0A      el.numbering,\0A      ..counter(eq).at(el.location())\0A    )\0A  } else {\0A    // Other references as usual.\0A    it\0A  }\0A}\0A\0A= Beginnings <beginning>\0AIn @beginning we prove @pythagoras.\0A$ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $ <pythagoras>\0A```" }>, align 1
