@anon.152f57749fcdf0a673d809c4f4545e7f.860 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1400 x i8] }> <{ [1400 x i8] c"Describes a kind of value.\0A\0ATo style your document, you need to work with values of different kinds:\0ALengths specifying the size of your elements, colors for your text and\0Ashapes, and more. Typst categorizes these into clearly defined _types_ and\0Atells you where it expects which type of value.\0A\0AApart from basic types for numeric values and [typical]($int)\0A[types]($float) [known]($str) [from]($array) [programming]($dictionary)\0Alanguages, Typst provides a special type for [_content._]($content) A value\0Aof this type can hold anything that you can enter into your document: Text,\0Aelements like headings and shapes, and style information.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#let x = 10\0A#if type(x) == int [\0A  #x is an integer!\0A] else [\0A  #x is another value...\0A]\0A\0AAn image is of type\0A#type(image(\22glacier.jpg\22)).\0A```\0A\0AThe type of `10` is `int`. Now, what is the type of `int` or even `type`?\0A```example\0A#type(int) \\\0A#type(type)\0A```\0A\0A# Compatibility\0AIn Typst 0.7 and lower, the `type` function returned a string instead of a\0Atype. Compatibility with the old way will remain for a while to give package\0Aauthors time to upgrade, but it will be removed at some point.\0A\0A- Checks like `{int == \22integer\22}` evaluate to `{true}`\0A- Adding/joining a type and string will yield a string\0A- The `{in}` operator on a type and a dictionary will evaluate to `{true}`\0A  if the dictionary has a string key matching the type's name" }>, align 1
